-- and the degeneracy of the atom map, as a theorem about every map rather than about one. An atom map of a
-- reaction says which atom of the product each atom of the reactant became. Take any such map on citrate
-- that tells its two arms' methylene hydrogens apart -- which any map must, since they end up in different
-- places -- and compose it with the arm exchange: the result is another map, and it differs from the first
-- at that atom. So the reactant's own symmetry gives at least two distinct maps for every map, and nothing
-- in the bond graph chooses between them.
--
-- This is the reaction-side form of the same defect, and it is what a metabolite annotation needs and does
-- not have. A reaction record that stores one map with no field saying which of the two it is does not
-- determine which atom became which; a record that stores the pair determines nothing either. What chooses
-- is the enzyme, because in its chiral environment the arm exchange is not an admitted operation -- which
-- is to say the map belongs to the reaction in its environment and not to the pair of molecules.
map-degenerate : (f : Nat → Nat) → (f 7 ≡ f 13 → Empty) → (f 7 ≡ f (cit-arms 7) → Empty)
map-degenerate f distinct = λ q → distinct q
